<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Sacred · Celebration · c. 1650 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">God Rest Ye Merry, Gentlemen</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>An old English Christmas carol, found in a manuscript of the 1650s, that wishes everyone peace and joy at Christmas.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>traditional</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>traditional</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>English</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>England</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>a manuscript of the early 1650s; first printed c. 1760</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Sacred · Celebration</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (traditional English carol; in a manuscript of the 1650s, printed c. 1760)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/god-rest-ye-merry-gentlemen">God Rest Ye Merry, Gentlemen</a>&quot;, also sung as &quot;God Rest You Merry, Gentlemen&quot; and known as &quot;Tidings of Comfort and Joy&quot;, is a traditional English Christmas carol. An early version is in an anonymous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> of the 1650s, with a slightly different first line. The earliest known printed edition is a broadsheet of about 1760, Three New Christmas Carols, and the November 1764 issue of the Monthly Review refers to it. Some sources take the carol back to the 16th century, while others date it to the 18th or early 19th century.</p><p>The opening words carry an older sense. &quot;God rest you merry&quot; meant &quot;may God keep you in peace and happiness&quot;: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a> English Dictionary records the phrase from 1534, and it appears in Shakespeare's As You Like It and, as &quot;rest you merry&quot;, in Romeo and Juliet, both of the 1590s. As that use of &quot;rest&quot; faded, the line came to be read and punctuated as &quot;God rest you, merry gentlemen&quot;, already in a variant printed in 1775 in The Beauties of the Magazines, taken from a song sheet bought from a street caroller. The form &quot;ye&quot; is a later pseudo-archaism.</p><p>The tune now best known is a traditional English melody in the minor mode. James Nares wrote it down in the mid-18th century as &quot;The old Christmas Carol&quot;; it first appears in print in a rondo for fortepiano by Samuel Wesley, already reviewed in 1815, and then in a parody published by William Hone in 1820, and the full tune as sung today was published by Chappell in 1855. A second tune, &quot;Cornish&quot;, was in print by 1833 and was called &quot;the usual version&quot; by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a> Book of Carols of 1928, but it is seldom heard now. In March 1824 The Gentleman's Magazine complained that no carols were heard in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> &quot;excepting some croaking ballad-singer bawling out 'God rest you, merry gentlemen'&quot;. The solicitor and antiquarian William Sandys did most to make the carol popular, by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> it in his Christmas Carols Ancient and Modern (1833).</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-dickens">Charles Dickens</a> refers to it in A Christmas Carol (1843), changing &quot;rest&quot; to &quot;bless&quot;, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-eliot">George Eliot</a> quotes it in Silas Marner (1861). Gustav Holst used it in his choral fantasy Christmas Day (1910), and Victor Hely-Hutchinson made the third movement of his Carol Symphony (1927) a scherzo on its tune. In the UK the text in Carols for Choirs (1961) has become the usual one. It has been recorded by many singers and bands since Bing Crosby's version of 1942.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A Christmas carol that wishes its hearers peace and happiness, in the old sense of its opening words, and brings them the good news of Christmas. The mood is sturdy and festive, though the best-known tune is in a minor key.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">The words</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>God rest you merry, gentlemen,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Let nothing you dismay,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>For Jesus Christ our Saviour</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Was born upon this day,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>To save us all from Satan's power</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>When we were gone astray.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>O tidings of comfort and joy,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>For Jesus Christ our Saviour was born on Christmas day.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>In Bethlehem in Jury</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>This blessed babe was born,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And laid within a manger</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Upon this blessed morn;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>The which his mother Mary</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Nothing did take in scorn.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>O tidings, &amp;c.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>From God our Heavenly Father</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>A blessed Angel came,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And unto certain Shepherds</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Brought tidings of the same,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>How that in Bethlehem was born</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>The Son of God by name.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>O tidings, &amp;c.</span></p></div><details class="sg-more"><summary>Show all 50 lines</summary><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Fear not, then said the Angel,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Let nothing you affright,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>This day is born a Saviour</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Of virtue, power, and might;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>So frequently to vanquish all</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>The friends of Satan quite.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>O tidings, &amp;c.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>The Shepherds at those tidings</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Rejoiced much in mind.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And left their flocks a feeding</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>In tempest, storm, and wind.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And went to Bethlehem straightway,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>This blessed babe to find.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>O tidings, &amp;c.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>But when to Bethlehem they came,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Whereas this infant lay,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>They found him in a manger</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Where oxen feed on hay,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>His mother Mary kneeling</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Unto the Lord did pray.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>O tidings, &amp;c.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Now to the Lord sing praises,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>All you within this place,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And with true love and brotherhood</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Each other now embrace;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>This holy tide of Christmas</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>All others doth deface.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>O tidings, &amp;c.</span></p></div></details></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Words: William Sandys, Christmas Carols Ancient and Modern (1833). Text from <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Christmas_Carols%2C_Ancient_and_Modern%2FGod_Rest_You_Merry%2C_Gentlemen" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p></div></section>
</div>
